# Lab Walkthrough: Add and Manage Guest Users

---

## Exercise 1 — Invite a Single Guest User

### Step 1 — Sign in to Microsoft Entra Admin Center

1. Go to **entra.microsoft.com**
2. Sign in with an account assigned either:
   - A **limited administrator directory role**, OR
   - The **Guest Inviter role**

> 💡 **SC-300 Tip:** The Guest Inviter role is a least-privilege role that allows
> a user to invite external guests without granting any other administrative permissions.
> Always follow the principle of least privilege — assign Guest Inviter rather than
> User Administrator when the only requirement is inviting guests.

---

### Step 2 — Navigate to Users

1. In the left navigation, select **Identity**
2. Under **Users**, select **All Users**

---

### Step 3 — Create a Guest Invitation

1. Select **+ New user** from the top menu
2. Select **Invite external user** from the dropdown

---

### Step 4 — Fill in Guest User Details

On the New user (Invite user) page, enter the following:

| Field | Value | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Email | Guest's individual email address | No group emails; no + symbols |
| Display name | Guest's full name | How they appear in your directory |
| Message | Optional personal message | Included in the invitation email |

### Email Address Restrictions

| ❌ Not Supported | ✅ Supported |
|---|---|
| Group email addresses (e.g. team@company.com) | Individual email (e.g. john@partner.com) |
| Plus symbols (e.g. john+work@company.com) | Standard email format only |
| Aliases with + characters | name@domain.com format |

> ⚠️ **Why no plus symbols?**
> Microsoft Entra ID does not currently support plus symbols in email addresses.
> If a user has a plus symbol in their address, omit the plus symbol and any
> characters between it and the @ symbol to avoid delivery issues.
> Example: `john+filter@company.com` → use `john@company.com`

---

### Step 5 — Send the Invitation

1. Review the details
2. Select **Invite**
3. The invitation email is sent automatically to the guest's email address

---

### Step 6 — Verify Guest Account in Directory

1. Return to **Identity → Users → All Users**
2. Find your guest account in the list
3. Check the **User type** column — it should show **Guest**

### What Happens After Invitation

```
You send invitation
        ↓
Guest receives email with link
        ↓
Guest clicks "Accept invitation"
        ↓
Guest authenticates with their own identity
(Microsoft account, Google, or their org's Entra ID)
        ↓
Guest account becomes Active in your directory
        ↓
Guest can access resources you have shared with them
```

---

## Exercise 2 — Bulk Invite Guest Users

Use this method when you need to invite **many guests at once** — partners,
vendors, or event attendees — without sending individual invitations one by one.

### Step 1 — Navigate to Bulk Operations

1. Sign in to **entra.microsoft.com** as a **User Administrator**
2. Navigate to **Identity → Users → All Users**
3. On the All users screen, select **Bulk operations** from the top menu
4. Select **Bulk invite**

---

### Step 2 — Download the CSV Template

1. In the **Bulk invite users** pane, select **Download to a sample CSV template**
2. Open the downloaded CSV file in a spreadsheet editor (Excel, Google Sheets)
3. Review the template structure — it contains two required columns:

| Column | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `Email address to invite` | ✅ Yes | Individual email of the guest to invite |
| `Redirection url` | ✅ Yes | URL guest is redirected to after accepting invitation |

See [bulk-invite-template.md](./bulk-invite-template.md) for the full CSV structure
and example data.

---

### Step 3 — Complete the CSV File

1. Add one row per guest user you want to invite
2. Fill in the email address and redirection URL for each guest
3. Ensure:
   - No group email addresses
   - No plus (+) symbols in email addresses
   - Redirection URL is a valid https:// URL (e.g. your company portal or Teams)
4. Save the completed CSV file

---

### Step 4 — Upload the CSV File

1. Return to the **Bulk invite users** pane in Entra admin center
2. Under **Upload your CSV file**, click **Browse**
3. Select your completed CSV file
4. Validation starts automatically when the file is selected

### Validation Results

| Result | Meaning | Action |
|---|---|---|
| ✅ "File uploaded successfully" | File is valid — ready to submit | Proceed to submit |
| ❌ Validation errors listed | Issues found in the CSV | Fix errors and re-upload |

**Common validation errors:**
- Invalid email format
- Missing required column
- Group email address detected
- Plus symbol in email address
- Invalid or missing redirection URL

---

### Step 5 — Submit the Bulk Operation

1. After successful validation, select **Submit**
2. Azure begins processing the bulk invite operation
3. Invitations are queued and sent to all listed guests

---

### Step 6 — Monitor the Bulk Operation Status

Two ways to check progress:

**Option A — View status directly:**
Select **"View the status of each operation"** link shown after submission

**Option B — Bulk operation results:**
Navigate to **Users → Bulk operation results** in the Activity section

### Reading the Results

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| # Success | Invitations sent successfully |
| # Failure | Invitations that failed — click to see reasons |
| Total Requests | Total rows submitted from your CSV |

When complete, a notification confirms **"The bulk operation succeeded"**

---

## Exercise 3 — Manage Guest Users (B2B Collaboration Demo)

This exercise uses an interactive click-through demo from Microsoft Learn:
🔗 [Manage Guest User Access in Azure AD for B2B Collaboration](https://mslearn.cloudguides.com/guides/Manage%20Guest%20User%20Access%20in%20Azure%20AD%20for%20B2B%20Collaboration)

### What the Demo Covers

**1. Invite external users to collaborate**
- Send B2B invitations to external partners
- View the invitation state in the directory
- Understand the guest acceptance flow

**2. Assign resources to guest users**
- Add guest users to groups
- Assign applications to guest users
- Share SharePoint sites with external guests

**3. Create Conditional Access policies for guest users**
- Create a CA policy targeting guest and external users specifically
- Require MFA for all guest sign-ins
- Restrict guest access to specific apps or locations
- Apply stricter session controls for external users

### Key Conditional Access Concepts for Guest Users

| Policy Setting | Recommended for Guests |
|---|---|
| Require MFA | ✅ Always — guests use external identities |
| Session sign-in frequency | Shorter timeout than internal users |
| Persistent browser session | Disabled for guests |
| Compliant device | Optional — guests may use unmanaged devices |
| Terms of use | Require guests to accept before accessing resources |

---

## Summary of All Exercises

| Exercise | Method | Invited | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| Single guest invite | Entra admin center — Invite external user | 1 user | Guest account in directory |
| Bulk guest invite | CSV upload via Bulk operations | Multiple users | All guests invited at once |
| Manage guest access | Conditional Access + resource assignment | All guests | Secure, governed access |

---

## B2B vs B2C — Know the Difference (SC-300 Exam)

| | B2B Collaboration | B2C (External Identities) |
|---|---|---|
| **Who** | Partners, vendors, contractors | Customers / consumers |
| **Identity** | Guest uses their own org identity | Customer creates their own account |
| **User type** | Guest | Customer (separate tenant) |
| **Use case** | Internal app sharing with partners | Public-facing customer apps |
| **Product** | Microsoft Entra External ID (B2B) | Microsoft Entra External ID (B2C) |
| **Admin center** | Entra admin center | Separate B2C tenant |
